  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" id="shapes" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-line">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <p class="mono-label mb-3">Step 03</p>
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">Two views of the same day</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <div class="mt-8 measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              The main screen is the Voiceboard: one row per child, one column per event
              type, for a single day. A provider scanning twelve children needs the rows
              to <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">scroll left and right together</strong>,
              so they stay lined up on the same point in time. Without that, the grid is
              twelve unrelated timelines that happen to be stacked.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-10 rounded-lg border border-line p-6 md:p-8">
            <p class="mono-label mb-4">How these sheets were made</p>
            <div class="space-y-4 text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
              <p>
                Every screen went through three passes: a rough sketch to work out the
                shape, a clean wireframe to fix it, and that same wireframe with the
                reasoning written onto it. The decisions themselves came from the
                provider calls described above, made while the app was being built. The
                sheets were drawn afterward, to document that reasoning for this case
                study.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>

    <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
      <p class="mono-label mb-6">How a screen got built</p>
      <FidelityBand
        title={screen('01-voiceboard').title}
